{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem demands an argument and a causal mechanism relating decentralized resilience to the 'strictly more total internal redundancy' of a centralized service. SRC_001 introduces a 'Diversity Index' and a 'Coupling Coefficient' but provides no causal derivation tying these quantities to the total-internal-redundancy asymmetry; the claim is a definitional threshold ('more resilient only if Diversity Index exceeds Coupling Coefficient') with no mechanism explaining why that comparison governs correlated-failure resilience. The biological-colony analogy is acknowledged as disanalogous (procurement vs. mutation) and its single overturn condition (a shared biological receptor) is irrelevant to software correlated failure, so the analogy cannot carry the causal load. Under a demand for a causal mechanism, an asserted inequality between named indices is not a mechanism.","target":"00628b0fd7a5d8c751d93c934c13eaa056f60ea0e4ababea11cc33c705f8738b"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_002 argues against decentralization by positing 'Cascading Correlation' via load-shifting, but its own countercondition concedes the same cascade can occur in the large service when its load balancer becomes the bottleneck. It then gives no causal reason why the decentralized path is worse when the centralized path has an equivalent, explicitly listed cascade mechanism. Since the problem's comparison is specifically against a large service with strictly more total internal redundancy, an argument that identifies a shared failure mode but does not establish which architecture is more vulnerable to it fails to resolve the claim in either direction; the mechanism is symmetric and thus non-discriminating.","target":"46dbea993d9813233671aad6e736a0b8b2439556b4550fb29e31909263c9d4be"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"4ecf96c929dbc68b28c2a5bd0efaf3e3893b36be1fa2828e5aa669c80322be8f"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_004 claims decentralized networks are 'fundamentally more fragile' to thundering-herd effects, but its mechanism is only that decentralized systems lack centralized admission control; it never causally compares this to a centralized service whose internal redundancy is strictly greater. The centralized service could implement the same admission-control and throttling internally, and SRC_004's own countercondition admits the centralized service can produce its own internal thundering herd during recovery. Without a mechanism that accounts for the centralized service's greater total internal redundancy (e.g., why that redundancy cannot absorb the retry storm), the conclusion of 'fundamentally more fragile' is unsupported.","target":"7dbb4c3d04f858a3ec9f2d13f697d841b6564ba6d63d1ee033bc2c959477ff3d"}]}